WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 220: Italy: The Italian Renaissance: Leonardo da Vinci, Michelangelo, and the Medici

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.640
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.560
[Rebecca] Today we enter the sunlit piazzas and marble studios of Italy.

4
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:19.520
[Rebecca] In the 15th century, Italian artists and patrons broke the medieval mold, rediscovering human beauty, perspective, and classical wisdom.

5
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:28.720
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the Italian Renaissance, the Medici, Leonardo da Vinci, and Michelangelo.

6
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:43.200
[George] In the 14th century, as Europe was recovering from the devastating trauma of the Black Death, the independent merchant city-state of Florence in Tuscany became the birthplace of a cultural revolution known as the Renescimento, the Rebirth.

7
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:54.160
[George] Situated along major European trade routes, Florence was a bustling mercantile republic dominated by powerful craft guilds, wool merchants, and international bankers.

8
00:00:54.160 --> 00:01:08.400
[George] Following the fall of Constantinople to the Ottoman Turks in 1453, Greek scholars fled west to Italy, carrying ancient manuscripts of Plato, Aristotle, and Homer that had been lost to Western Europe for centuries.

9
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:23.120
[George] Scholars like Petrarch and Marsilio Ficino pioneered humanism, a philosophical movement that emphasized human dignity, individual potential, and classical literature, reconciling pagan philosophy with Christian theology.

10
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:36.400
[George] In architecture, Filippo Brunelleschi engineered the crowning wonder of Florence, the monumental octagonal dome of Santa Maria del Fiore, spanning 143 feet without wooden centering.

11
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:43.040
[George] Proving that modern human ingenuity could equal and surpass the greatest architectural wonders of ancient Rome.

12
00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:53.520
[George] In the 15th century, along the banks of the Arno River, Florence ignited the artistic, scientific, and cultural rebirth of Europe, the Italian Renaissance.

13
00:01:53.520 --> 00:02:09.640
[George] Fueled by thriving wool guilds, international textile commerce, and the rediscovery of lost classical Greek and Roman manuscripts, Florentine artists turned away from medieval scholasticism to celebrate human reason, proportion, and natural beauty.

14
00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:16.440
[George] The economic engine that financed this cultural explosion was a single dynasty, the House of Medici.

15
00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:28.520
[George] Beginning with Giovanni di Bixai de Medici, who established the Medici Bank with branches across Rome, Venice, Geneva and London, the family became the official bankers to the papacy.

16
00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:43.480
[George] Giovanni's son, Cassimo the Elder, used his immense financial leverage to become the de facto ruler of Florence, while instituting a revolutionary policy of civic philanthropy, pouring gold into libraries, churches and public art.

17
00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:53.320
[George] The golden age of the dynasty arrived under Cassimo's grandson, Lorenzo de Medici, celebrated as Il Magnifico, Lorenzo the Magnificent.

18
00:02:53.320 --> 00:03:06.360
[George] Ruling Florence from 1469 to 1492, Lorenzo was a poet, diplomat, and passionate patron of the arts who turned his palace and sculpture garden into an open academy for young talent.

19
00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:22.520
[George] Lorenzo recognized the teenage Michelangelo, inviting him to live in the Medici Palace like a son, dining at his table alongside humanists and poets, establishing an enduring model of artistic patronage that transformed Florence into the cultural capital of Europe.

20
00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:31.960
[George] Behind this artistic explosion stood the Medici dynasty, wealthy merchant bankers who transformed Florence into the cultural capital of the Western world.

21
00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:48.960
[George] Led by Cassimo the Elder and his poet grandson Lorenzo the Magnificent, the Medici poured colossal fortunes into civic architecture, classical libraries and private academies, personally patronizing Brunelleschi, Donatello, Botticelli, and the young Michelangelo.

22
00:03:49.200 --> 00:04:05.680
[George] Born out of wedlock in the Tuscan hill town of Vinci in 1452, Leonardo da Vinci embodied the supreme ideal of the Renaissance man, a universal genius who believed that seeing, observation, and direct experience were the only true avenues to knowledge.

23
00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:21.520
[George] Apprenticed in Florence to the master Andrea del Verracchio, Leonardo quickly surpassed his teacher, painting an angel in Veracchio's baptism of Christ with such luminous, ethereal grace that the master reportedly put down his brushes in humble retirement.

24
00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:43.040
[George] Left-handed and writing in mirror script from right to left to prevent smudging his ink, Leonardo filled thousands of notebook pages with groundbreaking studies in human anatomy, bird flight, botany, optics, hydraulics, and mechanical engineering, sketching flying machines, armored tanks, and parachutes centuries ahead of their time.

25
00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:46.560
[George] Leonardo saw no distinction between art and science.

26
00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:57.520
[George] His anatomical dissections of human corpses in hospital morgues informed the lifelike posture and muscular vitality of his figures, creating art that pulsed with living breath.

27
00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:10.160
[George] Born in the Tuscan village of Vinci in 1452, Leonardo da Vinci was the quintessential Renaissance polymath, painter, sculptor, architect, engineer, and visionary anatomist.

28
00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:27.600
[George] In his sprawling, mirror script notebooks, Leonardo sketched revolutionary inventions centuries ahead of his time, flying machines, armored tanks, submarines, and hydraulic pumps, while dissecting human corpses by candlelight to understand the hidden biomechanics of the body.

29
00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:45.640
[George] In 1495, in the refectory of the convent of Santa Maria del Grazi in Milan, Leonardo painted his masterpiece, The Last Supper, depicting the psychological drama of the precise moment Christ reveals that one of the twelve will betray him, Leonardo captured the astonishment.

30
00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:54.280
[George] Guilt, and horror rippling through the apostles, organizing the composition with mathematical linear perspective radiating from Christ's head.

31
00:05:54.280 --> 00:06:02.280
[George] Around 1503, back in Florence, Leonardo began a portrait of Lisa Gherardini, the wife of a silk merchant.

32
00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:04.920
[George] Known to the world as the Mona Lisa.

33
00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:21.480
[George] Employing his revolutionary technique of esfumato, meaning smoked, Leonardo blended colors and tones without visible brushstrokes or harsh lines, creating the enigmatic, shifting smile and atmospheric background that captivated observers for centuries.

34
00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:37.480
[George] Leonardo never parted with the small oil painting, carrying it with him across the Alps to France, where he spent his final years as first painter, engineer, and architect to the king under the patronage of King Francis I, dying in ambus in 1519.

35
00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:50.440
[George] In Milan, Leonardo painted the Last Supper on the refectory wall of Santa Maria del Grazi, capturing the psychological drama of the twelve disciples at the exact moment Christ reveals that one will betray him.

36
00:06:50.440 --> 00:07:06.760
[George] Years later in Florence, Leonardo began the portrait of Lisa Gherardini, the Mona Lisa, pioneering the revolutionary sfumato technique of smoky, imperceptible transitions of light and shadow that bestowed upon her smile an eternal, enigmatic soul.

37
00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:17.760
[George] Where Leonardo was elegant, charming and scientific, his younger rival Michelangelo Buonarotti was brooding, solitary, intensely pious, and fiercely proud.

38
00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:28.880
[George] Born in Caprazi in 1475 and raised near stone quarries, Michelangelo insisted that his art came from nursing stonecutters' dust with his wet nurse's milk.

39
00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:33.840
[George] Michelangelo viewed himself not primarily as a painter, but as a sculptor.

40
00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:43.040
[George] Believing that every block of marble contained a living divine form waiting to be released, I saw the angel in the marble and carved until I set him free.

41
00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:50.080
[George] In 1499, at just twenty four years old, Michelangelo completed the breathtaking Pieta Incin.

42
00:07:50.080 --> 00:08:01.280
[George] Peter's in Rome, carving the grieving Virgin Mary holding the lifeless body of Christ from a single block of Carrara marble with such delicate, polished perfection that observers wept.

43
00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:08.000
[George] It was the only sculpture Michelangelo ever signed, carving his name into the strap across Mary's breast.

44
00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:22.160
[George] In 1501, returning to Florence, Michelangelo accepted an impossible commission, carving a colossal statue of David from a massive, damaged 18-foot block of marble that other sculptors had abandoned for forty years.

45
00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:30.880
[George] If Leonardo was the versatile philosopher of nature, Michelangelo Buonarotti was the fiery, tormented titan of marble sculpture.

46
00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:48.880
[George] Apprenticed in the Medici Gardens, Michelangelo believed that every block of white Carrara marble contained a living figure waiting to be liberated by his hammer and chisel, sleeping in his clothes with marble dust in his beard, and driving himself through sheer physical agony to create immortal masterpieces.

47
00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:59.280
[George] Working in solitary obsession behind high wooden palisades for three years, Michelangelo carved David, a masterpiece that redefined classical heroism.

48
00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:11.000
[George] Departing from medieval tradition that depicted David after the victory standing over Goliath's severed head, Michelangelo depicted the shepherd youth in the tense, focused moment before the battle.

49
00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:27.640
[George] Standing in classical contraposto, with his sling resting casually over his left shoulder and his stone gripped in his massive right hand, David's bulging neck veins, flared nostrils, and furrowed brow radiate intellectual concentration and moral resolve.

50
00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:44.680
[George] Unveiled in September 1504 outside the Palazzo della Signoria, the seventeen-foot marble giant became the supreme civic symbol of Florentine liberty, representing the small, free republic's unyielding defiance against tyrannical foreign monarchs and the Medici tyrants.

51
00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:53.800
[George] Contemporaries declared that Michelangelo possessed terribilità, an awe-inspiring, terrifying creative power that made him seem more than mortal.

52
00:09:53.800 --> 00:10:04.040
[George] In 1501, the 26-year-old Michelangelo took on a colossal, flawed block of marble nicknamed the giant that other sculptors had abandoned as ruined.

53
00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:10.840
[George] Working in secrecy behind wooden screens for three years, he carved the 18-foot-tall statue of David.

54
00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:22.360
[George] Capturing the biblical hero not after the battle, but in the tense, coiled moment before casting his stone, creating the ultimate emblem of Florentine's civic liberty and human dignity.

55
00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:36.360
[George] In 1508, the formidable warrior Pope Julius I summoned Michelangelo to Rome with a monumental, unwanted task: painting the curved, 10,000 square foot barrel vault of the Sistine Chapel ceiling.

56
00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:39.240
[George] Michelangelo resisted, protesting.

57
00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:42.120
[George] I am not a painter, I am a sculptor.

58
00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:51.600
[George] Suspecting that his architectural rival Bramante had suggested the commission, hoping he would fail, Michelangelo dismissed his assistance and locked the chapel doors.

59
00:10:51.600 --> 00:11:08.640
[George] For four agonizing years, from 1508 to 1512, standing on wooden scaffolding 60 feet above the stone floor, craning his neck backward with wet fresco plaster dripping into his eyes, Michelangelo painted the epic history of human creation from Genesis.

60
00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:21.920
[George] The central panel, the creation of Adam, became the most iconic image in Western art, the outstretched finger of the creator reaching across the infinite void to spark divine life into the relaxed, waiting finger of Adam.

61
00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:28.880
[George] When the scaffolding was finally dismantled on All Saints Day 1512, Rome stood paralyzed in wonder.

62
00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:40.240
[George] Michelangelo had transformed a chapel ceiling into a cosmic vision of human potential and divine majesty, establishing the Italian Renaissance as the radiant dawn of the modern world.

63
00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:43.600
[George] Commissioned by the tempestuous warrior Pope Julius I.

64
00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:54.960
[George] Michelangelo spent four grueling years on high wooden scaffolds beneath the vault of the Sistine Chapel, painting over 300 monumental figures, including the iconic creation of Adam.

65
00:11:54.960 --> 00:12:08.400
[George] Returning decades later to paint the terrifying last judgment behind the altar, Michelangelo, Leonardo, and their fellow Renaissance masters elevated the human spirit to breathtaking heights of sublime beauty.

66
00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:15.680
[Rebecca] The Italian Renaissance unlocked the divine genius of art, elevating human creativity to the heavens.

67
00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:23.360
[Rebecca] But for centuries, the land of Italy remained a fractured geographical expression of foreign-dominated kingdoms.

68
00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:27.840
[Rebecca] Next week, we conclude our Italian saga with episode 221.

69
00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:29.280
[Rebecca] The Risorgimento.

70
00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:44.680
[Rebecca] Giuseppe Garibaldi, The Thousand Redshirts, and United Italy, The Dream of Italian Unification, The Secret Societies of Mazzini, The Expedition of the Thousand in Sicily, The Meeting at Tino, and Rome proclaimed the eternal capital of Italy.

71
00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:49.000
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

